 {"id":"SPEC-AGENTIC","title":"Agentic memory loop spec (S1..S7: label, store, retrieve, delegate) \u2014 design authority for the agentic workstream","status":"complete","priority":"P1","complexity":2,"depends_on":[],"blocks":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"DESIGN AUTHORITY for the agentic workstream; filed complete because docs/specs/SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md exists in the tree (149 lines) and is committed with this workstream. Covers: (0) the measured audit that motivates it - every cycle is a fresh single-turn request (messages=[system,user], no history, no persisted transcript), _mem_notes is capped at 6 entries joined and truncated to 300 chars with a single _mem_goal string, src/core/ai_client.py supports native OpenAI function calling but the controller path never uses it, grep for subagent/delegate/research across cron_runner.py and src/core/ returns nothing, and the DuckBrain client (src/core/duckbrain_client.py, namespace pokemon-global) is used only for an end-of-run summary row and a boot-memory block; consequence in one number: map_topology is 1,219 of 1,352 escalations (90%). (1) Six principles. (2) The memory contract: namespaces pokemon-global and pokemon-run:<run_id>, the 8-label taxonomy (world/map/*, world/object/*, world/npc/*, world/path/*, world/dialog/*, world/mechanics/*, run/<run_id>/goal, run/<run_id>/failure/*), the per-write field set (key, domain, attributes, embedding_text, confidence, evidence, applies_when) and the three retrieval tools. (3) The bounded context-window contract (system, rolling window N=12, compressed summary, top-K=8 memory block, projection+screenshot; each with a char ceiling). (4) The 7-tool model-chosen surface. (5) The delegation contract (bounded subagent, capped finding, acceptance requires consumption in a LATER cycle). (6) Stages S1..S7 with their acceptance criteria and dependency order S1 -> S2 -> {S3,S4} -> S5 -> S6, S7 after S4. (7) Non-goals (no seeded story; not a JEV replacement; no unbounded transcript; benchmark meaning unchanged). Every other row in this workstream derives from this document; if code and the spec disagree, the spec wins until amended here.","created_at":"2026-09-26 00:45:46"}
 {"id":"MEM-API","title":"S1 memory API \u2014 a written fact is retrievable by key AND by label AND by similarity, after a process restart","status":"complete","priority":"P1","complexity":4,"depends_on":["SPEC-AGENTIC"],"blocks":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"STAGE S1 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"a written fact is retrievable by key AND by label AND by similarity, **after a process restart**\". Measured starting point from the spec's audit: the client src/core/duckbrain_client.py (namespace pokemon-global) already exposes remember / recall / get / list_keys / search, but the loop uses it only for an end-of-run summary row and a boot-memory block - the agent cannot label its own knowledge and pull it back on demand. The spec's retrieval surface is memory.get(key) exact, memory.query(labels=[...], limit=N) by label/domain, and memory.search(text, limit=N) by similarity, and retrieval must read the store rather than in-process state, which is what the restart clause pins down. ORDERED FIRST because every later stage reads memory: it is S1 in the spec's dependency chain S1 -> S2 -> {S3,S4} -> S5 -> S6, and S2's 'the NEXT cycle retrieves one' clause cannot be demonstrated before the read path exists.","created_at":"2026-09-26 00:45:46","worktree":"/home/kara/worktrees/ai_plays_poke-MEM-API","branch":"wt/MEM-API","worker_model":"gpt-5.6-sol","worker_provider":"openai-codex","worker_done":true,"work_commit":"88578a4","merge_commit":"3df4545"}
 {"id":"CTX-WIN","title":"S3 context window \u2014 the request carries prior turns, and the agent answers about a cycle >N back from the summary","status":"complete","priority":"P1","complexity":4,"depends_on":["MEM-POP"],"blocks":null,"primary_model":"gpt-5.6-sol","primary_provider":"openai-codex","fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"STAGE S3 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"the log shows prior turns in the request, and the agent answers about a cycle >N back from the summary\". Measured starting point from the spec: every cycle is a fresh single-turn request (messages=[{\"role\":\"system\",...},{\"role\":\"user\",...}] and nothing else), there is no message history and no persisted transcript anywhere in the repo, and the past survives only as _mem_notes (capped at 6 entries, joined and truncated to 300 chars) and one _mem_goal string plus the in-run results list that JEV/teacher read as 'recent events' - an event log, not a window. The target contract (spec section 3) assembles each request from a system block, a rolling window of the last N=12 (decision, action, result) triples, a compressed summary of earlier turns rebuilt as the window slides, and a top-K=8 relevant-memory block for the current map/tile/screen, each with its own character ceiling so per-cycle input cost stays predictable and the run cannot silently grow. ORDERED AFTER S2 because the relevant-memory block is populated from the store that S2 proves gets written; the spec places S3 (with S4) immediately after S2 in the chain S1 -> S2 -> {S3,S4} -> S5 -> S6.","created_at":"2026-09-26 00:45:46","worktree":"/home/kara/worktrees/ai-plays-poke-CTX-WIN","branch":"wt/CTX-WIN","dispatched_at":"2026-09-26T17:45:01-05:00","updated_at":"2026-09-26 19:24:24","worker_status":"complete","completed_at":"2026-09-26 19:00:00","work_commit":"72048dc","merge_commit":"228cc5e","worker_model":"gpt-5.6-sol","worker_provider":"openai-codex","worker_done":"true","worker_summary":"S3 context window landed in two legs: 07edaf0 rolling prior-turn window (N=12) + compressed summary + top-K memory block assembled into the overworld controller request; 72048dc (r2 judge remediation) routes the same window into the live JEV teacher request and logs it. Window present in request logs; history-coverage test added.","foreman_note":"T252 recovery tick 2026-09-26: verdict d928190d INCOMPLETE (evidence graded commit.patch of 6512ab6 \u2014 the prior waves worktree patch, 46KB \u2014 not this leg; r2 work 72048dc postdates the judge). r2-remediated work verified by foreman instead: full suite green on merged main, prior-turns wiring live in cron_runner+jev/teacher clients. Row closed on merged-state verification; verdict artifact folded with INCOMPLETE annotation."}
-{"id":"TOOLS-1","title":"S4 tool calling \u2014 the log shows >=1 model-chosen tool call with its result, and a failed call is visible to the model","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":5,"depends_on":["MEM-POP"],"blocks":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"STAGE S4 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"the log shows >=1 **model-chosen** tool call with its result; a failed call is visible to the model\". Measured starting point from the spec: src/core/ai_client.py does support native OpenAI function calling (tools param, tool_calls parsing, text-only-model fallback), but the controller path never uses it - it returns a JSON plan, a list of button presses - and _battle_tool_call only looks like tool use: it is a fixed translation table from JEV battle vocabulary (MOVE_n / RUN / SWITCH) to emulator transport calls, so the model is not choosing tools. Target surface (spec section 4): walk(direction, tiles), interact(target), read_dialog(), remember(key, domain, facts, applies_when), recall(key|labels|query), delegate_research(question, budget), set_goal(text, reason) / check_goal(). Rules that make the criterion checkable: the harness executes with verification, every call and its result is a decision row so the autonomy counters count tool use honestly, and a failed call surfaces its error to the model instead of silently degrading to a blind A-press. ORDERED AFTER S2 (not parallel with CTX-WIN) because two of the seven tools are remember/recall, which need the populated store and retrieval API to mean anything; S5 depends on this row because delegate_research is itself one of these model-chosen tools.","created_at":"2026-09-26 00:45:46"}
+{"id":"TOOLS-1","title":"S4 tool calling \u2014 the log shows >=1 model-chosen tool call with its result, and a failed call is visible to the model","status":"in_progress","priority":"P1","complexity":5,"depends_on":["MEM-POP"],"blocks":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"STAGE S4 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"the log shows >=1 **model-chosen** tool call with its result; a failed call is visible to the model\". Measured starting point from the spec: src/core/ai_client.py does support native OpenAI function calling (tools param, tool_calls parsing, text-only-model fallback), but the controller path never uses it - it returns a JSON plan, a list of button presses - and _battle_tool_call only looks like tool use: it is a fixed translation table from JEV battle vocabulary (MOVE_n / RUN / SWITCH) to emulator transport calls, so the model is not choosing tools. Target surface (spec section 4): walk(direction, tiles), interact(target), read_dialog(), remember(key, domain, facts, applies_when), recall(key|labels|query), delegate_research(question, budget), set_goal(text, reason) / check_goal(). Rules that make the criterion checkable: the harness executes with verification, every call and its result is a decision row so the autonomy counters count tool use honestly, and a failed call surfaces its error to the model instead of silently degrading to a blind A-press. ORDERED AFTER S2 (not parallel with CTX-WIN) because two of the seven tools are remember/recall, which need the populated store and retrieval API to mean anything; S5 depends on this row because delegate_research is itself one of these model-chosen tools.","created_at":"2026-09-26 00:45:46","worker_status":"dispatched","worktree":"~/worktrees/ai-plays-poke-TOOLS-1","branch":"wt/TOOLS-1","sessions":["ai-plays-poke-2026-10-01-03-02-26"]}
 {"id":"DELEG-1","title":"S5 delegation \u2014 >=1 delegated finding written to memory and consumed in a later cycle","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":4,"depends_on":["TOOLS-1"],"blocks":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"STAGE S5 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \">=1 delegated finding written to memory and **consumed in a later cycle**\" - and section 5 adds that acceptance requires the finding to be consumed in a LATER cycle, visible in the log, not asserted. Contract being implemented (spec section 5): delegate_research spawns a bounded subagent with max wall-clock, max tool calls and max cost; the subagent receives only the question plus declared public context and may search the web; it returns capped finding text, sources and confidence; the harness compresses the return into world/* or run/* memory and injects it into the parent's next context. Measured starting point from the spec: a grep for subagent/delegate/research across cron_runner.py and src/core/ returns nothing - there is no delegation wiring at all today. ORDERED AFTER TOOLS-1 because the only way to invoke delegation in this design is as a model-chosen tool call from the surface S4 lands, and its output must land through the memory path (S1/S2) to satisfy the 'written to memory' half of the criterion.","created_at":"2026-09-26 00:45:46"}
 {"id":"NAV-MEM","title":"S6 memory-driven navigation \u2014 using world/map/* + world/path/*, cycles-to-first-map-transition drops vs the S2 baseline, and the route is cited from memory","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":4,"depends_on":["CTX-WIN","MEM-PROJ"],"blocks":[],"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"STAGE S6 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"using `world/map/*` + `world/path/*`, cycles-to-first-map-transition drops vs the S2 baseline, and the route is cited from memory\". This is where the spec's headline number is spent: map_topology is 1,219 of 1,352 escalations (90%), i.e. the agent re-derives the same world fact every episode and pays a teacher call for it each time; the world/path/<from_map>-><to_map> label exists precisely to store a PROVEN traversable route plus the door tile, written when a map transition is observed, and world/map/<map_id> holds name, exits (tile + destination), landmarks and tiles visited. Both halves of the criterion are measurable: the cycle count must be compared against the baseline the S2 30-cycle run produces, and the route the agent takes must be traceable to a memory read rather than to a re-derivation. ORDERED AFTER BOTH CTX-WIN AND DELEG-1 because it consumes the window (the agent must see its own prior attempts within a run to avoid re-deriving them) and delegation (lookups about a map can arrive as a delegated finding), and it cannot be measured at all until MEM-POP has produced its baseline number; this matches the spec's chain S1 -> S2 -> {S3,S4} -> S5 -> S6. | QUORUM #1 fix 2026-09-26: dependency on DELEG-1 removed (web research is not a precondition for navigating an already-walked map) and MEM-PROJ added (memory must reach the JEV projection or this row's metric is structurally immune).","created_at":"2026-09-26 00:45:46"}
 {"id":"BENCH-PAR","title":"S7 benchmark parity \u2014 both --decision-mode jev and llm run the new loop, mode stamped, logs comparable","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":3,"depends_on":["TOOLS-1"],"blocks":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"STAGE S7 of SPEC_agentic_memory_loop.md. ACCEPTANCE (verbatim from the spec): \"both `--decision-mode jev` and `llm` run the new loop; mode stamped; logs comparable\". Existing state this builds on: the mode is already selectable (--decision-mode jev|llm, filed as event MODE-1 on 2026-09-26 with a live 3-cycle llm-mode proof where every row showed decision_mode=llm and jev_answered 0/3), and BENCH-1 is the standing row for the no-JEV benchmark whose meaning must not change - section 7 requires that the benchmark get the SAME tool surface, or that its logs say explicitly which surface it ran with. ORDERED AFTER TOOLS-1 per the spec's own dependency line ('S7 after S4'): parity is only measurable once there is a tool surface to stamp, and stamping it into every decision row is what keeps jev-mode and llm-mode logs comparable at all. P2 rather than P1 because it guards the benchmark's comparability instead of unblocking the loop itself.","created_at":"2026-09-26 00:45:46"}
